What Is Mint-to Logic™?
Mint-to Logic™ is a lifecycle enforcement protocol defined by Spencer Southern and governed under Unified Notice.

It controls the lifecycle of digital credentials, tokens, or permissions from issuance to expiration.
🔧 The Framework Includes (But Is Not Limited To):
Mint Units: Individually issued, lifecycle-bound credentials tied to a specific function, event, or access condition.

Validation Triggers: Rule-based behavioral or system-defined conditions that determine whether a Mint Unit progresses, pauses, or exits the lifecycle.

Exit States — Burn, Limbo, Code, or Use:

Burn: The credential is terminated permanently

Limbo: The credential is suspended or gated pending resolution

Code: The credential transitions into programmable function or logic execution

Use: The credential is acted upon or consumed by the end system

Modular Use: Any deployment of lifecycle logic — full or partial — triggers a modular usage fee, adjusted to match the scalability, variability, and scope of implementation.


